From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 17:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] HID: asus: bind the Vivobook S 16 S3607QA keyboard, 0B05:4543

The ASUS Vivobook S 16 S3607QA's I2C keyboard is 0B05:4543. hid-asus
doesn't list it, so hid-generic drives it. Only the volume keys work.
The other Fn keys send vendor usages nothing maps, the backlight can't
be set and stays lit through sleep, and key codes above 0x65, such as
the F23 a Copilot key sends, are dropped.

Its descriptor has the notebook keyboards' layout. The key array's
Logical Maximum of 0x65 sits at offset 54, a Consumer collection
declares usages up to 0x23c, and the vendor collection 0xff31 has input
report 0x5a and a 64-byte feature report 0x5a. Bind it with the I2C
keyboard quirks, the keyboard backlight and padded feature reports, as
for the 0B05:4B42. Fn-lock stays with the keyboard's firmware, since
nothing shows that this keyboard leaves it to the host.

The Zenbook A14 uses 0B05:4543 for its EC hotkey device instead, which
its tree names hdtl@17. Its descriptor isn't known. When the device has
no keyboard collection, keep its Consumer usages, so hotkeys it sends
that way still reach userspace.

Signed-off-by: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
---
 drivers/hid/hid-asus.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/hid/hid-ids.h  |  1 +
 2 files changed, 25 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/hid/hid-asus.c b/drivers/hid/hid-asus.c
index d8aaca2be..dc44227c5 100644
--- a/drivers/hid/hid-asus.c
+++ b/drivers/hid/hid-asus.c
@@ -1014,6 +1014,18 @@ static bool asus_has_report_id(struct hid_device *hdev, u16 report_id)
 	return false;
 }
 
+static bool asus_has_keyboard_application(struct hid_device *hdev)
+{
+	struct hid_report *report;
+
+	list_for_each_entry(report, &hdev->report_enum[HID_INPUT_REPORT].report_list, list) {
+		if (report->application == HID_GD_KEYBOARD)
+			return true;
+	}
+
+	return false;
+}
+
 static int asus_kbd_register_leds(struct hid_device *hdev)
 {
 	struct asus_drvdata *drvdata = hid_get_drvdata(hdev);
@@ -1610,6 +1622,14 @@ static int asus_probe(struct hid_device *hdev, const struct hid_device_id *id)
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The Zenbook A14 uses 0B05:4543 for its EC hotkey device rather than
+	 * its keyboard. Without a keyboard collection, keep its Consumer usages.
+	 */
+	if (hdev->product == USB_DEVICE_ID_ASUSTEK_I2C_VIVOBOOK_KEYBOARD &&
+	    !asus_has_keyboard_application(hdev))
+		drvdata->quirks &= ~QUIRK_NO_CONSUMER_USAGES;
+
 	/* Check for vendor for RGB init and handle generic devices properly. */
 	rep_enum = &hdev->report_enum[HID_INPUT_REPORT];
 	list_for_each_entry(rep, &rep_enum->report_list, list) {
@@ -1851,6 +1871,10 @@ static const struct hid_device_id asus_devices[] = {
 	  I2C_KEYBOARD_QUIRKS | QUIRK_FILTER_CAMERA_COMPANION |
 	  QUIRK_USE_KBD_BACKLIGHT | QUIRK_PAD_FEATURE_REPORTS |
 	  QUIRK_HID_FN_LOCK },
+	{ HID_I2C_DEVICE(USB_VENDOR_ID_ASUSTEK,
+		USB_DEVICE_ID_ASUSTEK_I2C_VIVOBOOK_KEYBOARD),
+	  I2C_KEYBOARD_QUIRKS | QUIRK_USE_KBD_BACKLIGHT |
+	  QUIRK_PAD_FEATURE_REPORTS },
 	{ HID_I2C_DEVICE(USB_VENDOR_ID_ASUSTEK,
 		USB_DEVICE_ID_ASUSTEK_I2C_TOUCHPAD), I2C_TOUCHPAD_QUIRKS },
 	{ HID_USB_DEVICE(USB_VENDOR_ID_ASUSTEK,
diff --git a/drivers/hid/hid-ids.h b/drivers/hid/hid-ids.h
index cb7f16d75..068ebfe61 100644
--- a/drivers/hid/hid-ids.h
+++ b/drivers/hid/hid-ids.h
@@ -221,6 +221,7 @@
 #define USB_DEVICE_ID_ASUSTEK_T304_KEYBOARD	0x184a
 #define USB_DEVICE_ID_ASUSTEK_I2C_KEYBOARD	0x8585
 #define USB_DEVICE_ID_ASUSTEK_I2C_ZENBOOK_KEYBOARD	0x4b42
+#define USB_DEVICE_ID_ASUSTEK_I2C_VIVOBOOK_KEYBOARD	0x4543
 #define USB_DEVICE_ID_ASUSTEK_I2C_TOUCHPAD	0x0101
 #define USB_DEVICE_ID_ASUSTEK_ROG_KEYBOARD1 0x1854
 #define USB_DEVICE_ID_ASUSTEK_ROG_KEYBOARD2 0x1837
